## Why can1 may be correct instead of can0

`can0` and `can1` are Linux interface names. They are not guaranteed to match the physical labels printed on the CAN HAT.

The HAT has two MCP2515 CAN chips. During boot, Linux detects them and assigns interface names.

Example:

```text
spi1.2 → can0
spi1.1 → can1
```

If your FLY Super Pro 8 board is physically connected to the HAT channel that Linux named `can1`, then `can1` is the correct interface.

That is why one interface may return nothing:

```text
can0 → Total 0 uuids found
```

while the other interface finds the board:

```text
can1 → Found canbus_uuid=xxxxxxxxxxxx, Application: Klipper
```

This can happen because of:

| Reason | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Linux probe order | Linux may name the first detected CAN chip `can0`, even if that is not the physical port you are using. |
| HAT silkscreen vs Linux naming | The printed CAN0/CAN1 label on the HAT does not always match the Linux interface name. |
| SPI chip-select mapping | The working CAN chip may be on `spi1.1` or `spi1.2`. |
| Physical cable position | The cable may be plugged into the connector that Linux names `can1`. |

So always run:

```bash
python3 ~/klipper/scripts/canbus_query.py can0
```

```bash
python3 ~/klipper/scripts/canbus_query.py can1
```

Then use whichever interface returns the UUID.

Do not edit Klipper randomly until the CAN query finds the board.
